																		Good idea, but...
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/th4tsmaname" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7008319); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7008319&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609096661" alt="Th4tsMaName" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7008319)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/th4tsmaname" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7008319); return false;" >Th4tsMaName</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609089394 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Dec 2020 17:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4855186">
									

<p>You will need to work on your writing style a bit.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;writing&nbsp;style</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;writing&nbsp;style</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>You do not have a big problem with scientific language, but there are some big scratches to polish out of existence.</p>
<p>Sometimes you use very good sounding words like &quot;homicide&quot; but in the very same sentence you say kids instead of children. Such minor informalities spoil the experience a bit. But there are some bigger concerns too.</p>
<p>Especially the lower part of the description sounds like a videogame.<br />
For example:<br />
&quot;a larger than normal strength level&quot;<br />
or<br />
&quot;It needs three medium- sized bullets to the head to be penetrated&quot;</p>
<p>As a self proclaimed weapons expert the second sentence gives me a headache. What are medium sized bullets? 7,62mm? 12,7mm? How thick has a skull made of bones to be for being impenetrable by one 12,7mm bullet? Where did the test shots hit? Front, Side, Back? You get the point. And what is a normal strength level? Why do these SCP-5580-2 have to be exactly 2 meters tall? Nature doesn't draw straight lines.</p>
<p>Don't get me wrong, its an fabulous story to tell at the ceasefire, but for the SCP-Universe it is just not scientific enough. Implant some figures (like the ones above), do some research on what you are scribing about. And if there are no exact figures, scribe: SCP-5580-2 are &quot;approximately&quot; 2 meters tall.</p>
<p>Furthermore try to ban the word &quot;and&quot; a bit. Proofreading, you will stumble upon sections, in which you have aight sentences in a row, connected with &quot;and&quot; to four sentences. This looks a bit unprofessional.</p>
<p>Finally I want to talk about what this SCP really has a problem with: It has no special context whatsoever. What many people need to learn is, that we are not reading these SCP-articles to experience thrilling brutality, or look at cool monsters. There are countless horror films that do a better job with that. We, the people reading about SCPs want a <span style="text-decoration: underline;">story</span>. Imagine a horror film without a story. There are such films out there, that give you two hours of scary dullness. You don't want that.</p>
<p>Here is an idea how you can make your SCPs story more unique, because I am pretty sure that someone already came up with something similar. There are over 5000 SCPs out there.</p>
<p>The story of discovery has some thrilling degree in it. Use the fact, that children often say exactly what they think. Make some interviews with them. Give them a major role in the story. Way more interesting than just a random dude. Or ad a researcher who started friendship-relations with the children and recovered them from the evil (or perhaps failed). Add some feeling, some character-development, some <span style="text-decoration: underline;">story</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

